// ============================================================================
// AI Routing Policies — THE provider-configuration table 
//
// ## What one row is
//
// **One row is ONE PROVIDER CONFIGURATION for one `(tenant, taskKey)`.** Many
// rows may exist for the same selection; they form the ORDERED CANDIDATE CHAIN,
// ascending by `priority`, and EXACTLY ONE of them may carry `isDefault = true`.
//
// That grain is new. shipped this table as one row per AUTHORED POLICY
// REVISION with the candidate list buried in `candidatesJson`, keyed
// `@@unique([tenantId, taskKey, policyVersion])`. re-grained it because
// the product requires many configurations per task with one elected default,
// and a JSON array cannot carry a foreign key, cannot be indexed, cannot be
// promoted between tenants row-by-row, and cannot be constrained to one default.
// The re-grain was safe to do in place: the table had ZERO runtime readers and
// ZERO rows (program finding F-8, confirmed against the dev DB).
//
// ## Owner reversal on record — OD-3 (2026-09-01)
//
// ruled that `AiTaskDefault` survives. **OD-3 explicitly reverses it.**
// `AiTaskDefault` is ABSORBED into this table: every one of its rows is migrated
// here as an `isDefault = true` candidate, and this table is the source of truth
// for "which provider + model serves task X for tenant Y". `AiTaskDefault` is
// retired — see the RETIRED banner on `ai-task-default.prisma` for the staged
// removal and what still reads it during the transition.
//
// ## Where it sits relative to its siblings — now by FOREIGN KEY, not by string
//
//   AiProviderConnection WHERE a provider lives + HOW to authenticate to it.
//   AiModel the model CATALOGUE a configuration selects from.
//   AiRuntimeProfile hyperparameters/concurrency per (provider, model).
//   AiRoutingPolicy ← THIS. Binds a task to (connection, model) with an
//                           order, an election and an on/off switch.
//
// Finding F-6 recorded that the provider-configuration unit did not exist as a
// row: it was a STRING-JOINED chain, `AiTaskDefault.taskKey → modelSlug` ⋈
// `AiModel.slug → provider` ⋈ `AiProviderConnection.(service, provider)`. Both
// string joins are replaced here by real FKs — `providerConnectionId` and
// `modelId`. A configuration can no longer name a connection or a model that
// does not exist, and a hard delete of either is refused by the database.
//
// ## The election — enforced by the DATABASE, keyed on `taskKey`
//
//   CREATE UNIQUE INDEX "AiRoutingPolicy_tenant_task_default_unique"
//     ON core."AiRoutingPolicy" ("tenantId", "taskKey")
//     WHERE "isDefault" = true AND "resourceStatus" != 'DELETED';
//
// Prisma's schema DSL cannot express a partial unique index, so it is
// hand-written in the migration — the same mechanism, and the same predicate
// shape, as `WorkflowDefinition_tenant_slug_active_unique`.
//
// **⚠ The index keys on `taskKey`, NOT `taskKind`, and this is load-bearing
// .** `taskKind` is deliberately COARSER: `TEXT_GENERATION` spans
// `text.live`, `text.finalize`, `text.test` and `harness.judge`; `PII_DETECTION`
// spans `guardrail.pii` and `guardrail.pii.spans`. An index on the KIND would
// make those mutually exclusive, so a tenant could not hold a default for both
// `text.live` and `text.finalize`. **The election is per SELECTION, not per
// kind.** Do not "simplify" it onto `taskKind`.
//
// A service-level guard ALONE is what produced finding F-7 in the first place,
// so the constraint lives in the database and the service merely makes the
// happy path atomic (`setDefault` unsets the incumbent and sets the successor
// inside one `runInTransaction`, so a caller never performs two writes).
//
// ## Resolution is the house cascade and nothing else
//
// Request tenant → SYSTEM, TWO tiers, widening only on ABSENCE. `50000000-…`
// ("Global") is a CUSTOMER tenant — the platform-admin playground — and must
// never appear in the cascade. Because the SYSTEM row is what every tenant
// without its own row must read, this model is registered in
// SYSTEM_SHARED_READ_MODELS as well as TENANT_SCOPED_MODELS: reads widen to
// [caller, SYSTEM], writes are NOT widened.
//
// ## No secrets on this model, and funding is DERIVED
//
// A row names a connection by FK; the credential itself stays in
// `AiProviderConnection.encryptedApiKey` as Vault-Transit ciphertext. Funding
// (BYOK vs CLOUD) is DERIVED from which TIER supplied the connection row
// (`row.tenantId === SYSTEM_TENANT_ID`), never stamped here — a call site that
// stamps it mis-bills silently.
// ============================================================================

model AiRoutingPolicy {
  // meta fields
  metaData Json?  @map("_metadata") @db.JsonB
  version  Int    @default(1) @map("_version")
  id       String @id @default(uuid(7))

  // multi tenant fields (SYSTEM row = platform default)
  tenantId String

  // core fields
  taskKey String // same vocabulary as AI_TASK_KEYS (e.g. text.live | text.finalize)

  // the canonical task taxonomy `taskKey` belongs to. DERIVED from
  // `taskKey` via AI_TASK_KIND_BY_TASK_KEY, never independently chosen.
  // Nullable so a row written by an un-migrated writer lands "unclassified"
  // rather than carrying a plausible-but-wrong default; selection is
  // fail-closed platform-wide, so "nobody classified this" must stay
  // distinguishable from "classified as X".
  taskKind AiTaskKind?

  // ─────────────── — the provider-configuration binding ───────────────

  // Human label for this configuration. Free text; it is what an admin sees in
  // the picker and what an export/import artifact is identified by. NOT a key.
  displayName String?

  // WHICH connection serves. Real FK — this replaces the `connectionRef` string
  // that used to live inside `candidatesJson`, and the `(service, provider)`
  // string join through `AiModel.provider` that F-6 described.
  //
  // Nullable ONLY so the column could be added to an existing table without a
  // default; every row this ticket's backfill writes carries one, and the
  // application refuses to elect a default whose connection is absent
  // (selection is `failMode: closed` — nothing is substituted).
  providerConnectionId String?

  // WHICH catalogue model. Real FK, replacing `AiTaskDefault.modelSlug`'s
  // by-slug reference. Same nullability rationale as `providerConnectionId`.
  modelId String?

  // The provider-side model identifier actually sent on the wire, when it
  // differs from the catalogue row's `slug` — an Azure OpenAI DEPLOYMENT name,
  // an LM Studio GGUF id, a vLLM served-model-name. NULL means "use the
  // AiModel's own identity".
  //
  // This is why the binding is (connection, model, modelRef) and not just
  // (connection, model): on Azure the unit of serving is a DEPLOYMENT and one
  // deployment binds one model, so "one configuration per task deployment" is
  // architecturally forced rather than a convenience.
  modelRef String?

  // The elected default for this (tenantId, taskKey). AT MOST ONE row per
  // selection may be true — enforced by the partial unique index described in
  // the file header, NOT by the application alone.
  isDefault Boolean @default(false)

  // Candidate on/off WITHOUT deleting the row, so an admin can park a
  // configuration (and its history) instead of destroying it. Distinct from
  // `resourceStatus` (lifecycle) and from `killSwitch` (operator stop button).
  enabled Boolean @default(true)

  // Promoted OUT of the JSON candidate onto the row, because the hard
  // gates read them on every fallback hop and a gate input that lives in
  // unindexed JSON cannot be reported on, filtered or constrained.
  //
  // `residency` is an OPAQUE LABEL supplied by configuration — the gate asks
  // only "is the hop's class EQUAL to the primary's", never "is class X
  // permitted", so there is no enumerated list of clouds in application code to
  // drift from reality. `baaCovered` is per-vendor AND per-model.
  residency  String?
  baaCovered Boolean?

  // Task-specific extras carried over from `AiTaskDefault.configJson`
  // (thresholds and the like). Absorbed verbatim by the backfill.
  configJson Json? @db.JsonB

  // ─────────────────────── authored-revision lineage ───────────────────────

  // The AUTHORED revision of this configuration — NOT the `_version` OCC
  // counter above. `_version` is owned by the database, bumped on every write,
  // and exists so a concurrent PATCH is rejected (`updateWithVersion`).
  //
  // ⚠ It is no longer part of a natural key. keyed the table
  // `@@unique([tenantId, taskKey, policyVersion])`, which was correct at the
  // one-row-per-revision grain and is WRONG at this one: many rows now share
  // `(tenantId, taskKey)` because that is what an ordered candidate chain IS.
  // That unique index is dropped by the migration. Keeping the column
  // preserves the supersede-only audit lineage of
  policyVersion Int @default(1)

  status               AiRoutingPolicyStatus  @default(DRAFT)
  strategy             AiRoutingStrategy      @default(PRIORITY)
  explicitProviderMode AiExplicitProviderMode @default(STRICT) // STRICT is the platform default

  // Position in the ordered candidate chain: LOWER SERVES FIRST. Also the
  // tie-break when two rows match equally specifically
  priority Int @default(0)

  // Operator stop button ( Defaults OFF, like every kill-switch.
  killSwitch Boolean @default(false)

  // nested structures, JSONB.
  matchJson Json? @db.JsonB // { models, metadata, minContextTokens, maxContextTokens }

  // ⚠ DEPRECATED by and NO LONGER READ BY THE RESOLVER.
  //
  // This carried the whole ordered chain as an array of
  // `{ rank, weight, connectionRef, model, residency, baaCovered, maxTtftMs }`
  // objects. That chain is now the SET OF ROWS sharing `(tenantId, taskKey)`,
  // ordered by `priority`, each with real FKs. Made nullable so the column can
  // be dropped in a follow-up without a second grain change; it is retained
  // only so an operator can still read what a earlier revision authored.
  //
  // Do NOT write it, and do NOT reintroduce a code path that reads it — two
  // homes for the candidate chain is exactly the string-joined fragmentation
  // F-6 recorded.
  candidatesJson Json? @db.JsonB

  fallbackJson Json? @db.JsonB // { maxDepth, triggers[], requireSameResidencyClass, requireBaaCovered, crossFundingAllowed }
  healthJson   Json? @db.JsonB // { consecutiveFailures, failureRatePct, windowSec, baseEjectionSec, maxEjectionSec, maxEjectionPct, halfOpenProbes, rateLimit }

  // Ceilings promoted OUT of JSON because they are queryable facts a super
  // admin sorts and reports on ("which policies cap streams below N?") and the
  // saturation panels read directly. NULL = no ceiling expressed here;
  // the AiRuntimeProfile cascade still applies.
  maxConcurrentStreams Int?
  requestsPerMinute    Int?
  tokensPerMinute      Int?

  // Cache-affinity / sticky-routing hints (prefix-cache hit rate is a
  // saturation panel). Nested and advisory, so JSONB rather than columns.
  affinityJson Json? @db.JsonB

  // Supersede-only lineage: the `policyVersion` this revision replaced,
  // and when it was promoted to ACTIVE. Both NULL on a first DRAFT.
  supersedesVersion Int?
  activatedAt       DateTime?

  // resource status fields
  resourceStatus          ResourceStatusType @default(ENABLED)
  resourceStatusUpdatedAt DateTime?
  resourceStatusUpdatedBy String?

  // audit fields
  createdBy String?  @default("60000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000")
  updatedBy String?
  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt

  // relations — the two FKs that replace F-6's string joins.
  //
  // `onDelete: Restrict` on both: a connection or a catalogue model that a live
  // configuration binds cannot be hard-deleted out from under it. The house
  // rule is soft delete anyway, so this closes the one path that bypasses it
  // (a stray admin script, a future call site that forgets).
  //
  // The FKs deliberately carry NO tenant column, because a TENANT row must be
  // able to bind a SYSTEM-tenant catalogue model or the SYSTEM platform
  // connection — that inheritance IS the two-tier cascade. Tenancy is enforced
  // by the tenant-scope extension and the service, not by the FK.
  providerConnection AiProviderConnection? @relation("AiRoutingPolicyConnection", fields: [providerConnectionId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  model              AiModel?              @relation("AiRoutingPolicyModel", fields: [modelId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  // indexing.
  //
  // On `@@unique`, `name:` is the CLIENT-facing compound key and `map:` is the
  // DATABASE index name; on `@@index`, `name:` IS the database name. Getting
  // that pair backwards is what drifted the ledger permanently in.
  //
  // There is NO `@@unique` here any more: the only uniqueness this table owns
  // is the DEFAULT ELECTION, which is partial (`WHERE isDefault = true AND
  // resourceStatus != 'DELETED'`) and therefore inexpressible in the Prisma
  // DSL. It lives in the migration as raw SQL.
  //
  // The composite indexes lead with `tenantId`, so they also serve the plain
  // tenant-scope reads the extension injects — no separate `[tenantId]` index.
  @@index([tenantId, taskKey], name: "AiRoutingPolicy_tenantId_taskKey_idx")
  // the by-kind read the unified selection surface is built on.
  @@index([tenantId, taskKind], name: "AiRoutingPolicy_tenantId_taskKind_idx")
  // "the elected default for this selection", the single hottest
  // read on the table (every resolution starts here).
  @@index([tenantId, taskKey, isDefault], name: "AiRoutingPolicy_tenantId_taskKey_isDefault_idx")
  // reverse lookups: "what breaks if I disable this connection /
  // retire this model?". Also what makes the FK's Restrict check cheap.
  @@index([providerConnectionId], name: "AiRoutingPolicy_providerConnectionId_idx")
  @@index([modelId], name: "AiRoutingPolicy_modelId_idx")
  @@schema("core")
}
